02:03.660 --> 02:06.270
And if we look in the .git folder,

02:06.270 --> 02:09.000
there is a directory called logs,

02:09.000 --> 02:10.380
and I can actually see it over here.

02:10.380 --> 02:13.530
I have the .git folder open in VS Code.

02:13.530 --> 02:16.110
Here it is, a logs directory.

02:16.110 --> 02:18.660
And then there's a file called HEAD.

02:18.660 --> 02:20.490
And this might be shocking,

02:20.490 --> 02:22.530
but this file is actually readable.

02:22.530 --> 02:26.220
It's not compressed, it's not binary, it's a lot of text.

02:26.220 --> 02:28.770
But what we have are a series of entries in here.

02:28.770 --> 02:32.070
So here's the first one and the second one

02:32.070 --> 02:32.903
and the third one.

02:32.903 --> 02:35.910
I'm pretty zoomed in, so it kind of makes it look

02:35.910 --> 02:37.350
worse than it is.

02:37.350 --> 02:40.470
But each one of these entries is a move

02:40.470 --> 02:42.598
for the HEAD reference.

02:42.598 --> 02:44.520
I'll just show you down at the bottom.

02:44.520 --> 02:47.580
HEAD, most recently, let's see,

02:47.580 --> 02:49.920
there was a new commit made and before that

02:49.920 --> 02:51.750
I actually did a Git reset.

02:51.750 --> 02:53.340
Before that, I did a commit,

02:53.340 --> 02:56.700
but then before that I moved from some commit,

02:56.700 --> 03:00.090
I was in detached HEAD, and I moved to master.

03:00.090 --> 03:02.790
So it's tracking when I switch branches.

03:02.790 --> 03:04.860
So instead of just going through a history,

03:04.860 --> 03:07.372
you weren't even present for any of this, really,

03:07.372 --> 03:10.620
